<section aria-labelledby="postgres-ai-example">
  <h2 id="postgres-ai-example">Example: diagnose a PostgreSQL lock incident</h2>
  <blockquote class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-brand-500 pl-5 text-zinc-200">
    Find the sessions blocking checkout queries. Report the blocking chain, query age,
    and affected sessions. Suggest a query to cancel only if the findings support it, then wait for my decision.
  </blockquote>
  <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5">
    <li>
      Read the blocking chains and check the sessions they identify. Confirm the process
      ID (PID) and current query before suggesting a change.
    </li>
    <li>
      Consider <a href="#action-postgres.cancel_query">query cancellation</a>
      if the findings support it. This stops the current query and keeps the connection open.
      <a href="#action-postgres.terminate_backend">Terminating a backend</a>
      disconnects
      the session. Both are high-risk actions with different consequences.
    </li>
    <li>
      Read the blocking chain again after the action finishes. Check whether the block
      cleared and report which sessions still need attention.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Set policy rules for the reads and changes the agent may make. Asking it to wait in a
    prompt gives it guidance. Requiring approval in policy makes it wait before running
    the action. See <.link navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}>how action policies and approvals work</.link>.
  </p>
</section>
